#{[#edb/ref 17592186050902 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534} :activity/id #uuid "10bdaba2-b2d6-5479-a66a-9f5c1d933300" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060897 :tutorial/content "The **second derivative**  is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x).$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x).$ After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x).$ So using prime $\\left('\\right)$ notation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'.\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function $f(x) = 4x^2 + 3x.$ The first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}+3x)^{'} \\\\  & =4(2)x+3 \\\\  & =8x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =({f}^{'}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(8x+3)^{'} \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :question/id #uuid "0bc72287-b782-5add-89ef-f2a465573ab0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3653" :question/problem "Find $f''(x)$ for $f(x) = 2x^3 + \\dfrac 1 x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =2(3){x}^{2}-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2})^{′} \\\\  & =6(2)x-(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =12x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060898 :tutorial/content "We can also express the second derivative using $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}$ notation, also known as Leibniz notation. If we have a function $f(x),$ then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x),$ in which case we could also express the second derivative as $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :tutorial/title "Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for $y = 3 x^4 + 5x+ \\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =3(4){x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3){x}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060899 :tutorial/content "To calculate the third derivative of the function $f(x)=2x^4+3x,$ we need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}+3x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1}+1\\cdot 3{x}^{1-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =8{x}^{3}+3{x}^{0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}+3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3{x}^{3-1}+0 \\\\ \\text{ } & =24{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot 2{x}^{2-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'''(x)=48x.$ \n\nUsing Leibniz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x).$ This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x).$" :tutorial/title "Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :question/id #uuid "627f1ef4-64a8-5764-89c7-cb4e31009dbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3655" :question/problem "Given $y = \\dfrac {3 x^2 + x - 1} x$, find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^{3} y} {\\textrm d x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as the sum of powers of $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =3x+1-{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1-{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+0-(-1){x}^{-2} \\\\  & =3+{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3+{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2(-3){x}^{-4} \\\\  & =6{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"}}] :activity/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059952}] [#edb/ref 17592186049119 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093327} :activity/id #uuid "bfd96d16-131a-5887-9f3c-822479cc8e6b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3 - x^2$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=px+q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the values of $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^3 - x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2})=3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1, 0){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093442 :tutorial/content "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{1cm} m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}.\n$$\n\nHere, $m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}$ because the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line, and $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $A(1,1).$ This normal line is the dashed line shown below, which is perpendicular to the solid tangent line. \n\n![](images/31/31ba09ff14e5bd485794f283c5d6a5b087b6c21fdb9242af01b75ec8e8744e0d.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nAt $A(1,1),$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nf'(1) \t= 2(1) = 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, we want the normal line, and the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(1)} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we work out the equation of the normal line using the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the normal line is $y=-\\dfrac x 2 + \\dfrac 3 2.$ We could also write this in standard form as $x+2y=3.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091953 :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-148" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^3 -x^2$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = 2x^3-x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x)  =6x^2 -2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 6(1)^2 - 2(1) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac 1 4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091954 :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x} -4x$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = (1)^{-1/2} - 4 = -3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = \\dfrac 1 3.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(7,0).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092461}] [#edb/ref 17592186049443 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106584} :activity/id #uuid "aa7f2ab0-ace7-558b-8680-463a0ed634bc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107146 :tutorial/content "Recall that for $x >0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{x}) = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this to all values $x\\neq 0$ by including an absolute value inside the natural logarithm: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{|x|}) = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral of the reciprocal function is\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln |x| + C.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105574 :question/id #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" :question/math-academy-id "e-375" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107147 :tutorial/content "For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C = \\ln K,$ where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+\\operatorname{ln}K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}}] :activity/title "Integrating the Reciprocal Function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105994}] [#edb/ref 17592186050884 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035} :activity/id #uuid "de17f2e1-7eda-5a05-b05f-fc9631097dd2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205174 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x \\\\ x+1\\,-\\,x & =x\\,-\\,x \\\\ (x-x)+1 & =(x-x) \\\\ 0+1 & =0 \\\\ 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0,$ is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true. \n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\nII. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\nIII. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205208 :tutorial/content "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form \n\n$$\nax = b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0.$\n\nThen, if $a = 0,$ the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =b \\\\ (0)x & =b \\\\ 0 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that $b \\neq 0.$ Therefore, the equation has no solution." :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have No Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205207 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/id #uuid "cba9196c-fdc3-594d-a1ad-948cf75bd366" :question/math-academy-id "e-17213" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4(r - 2)y = -8\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(r-2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4(r-2)}{4} & =\\frac{0}{4} \\\\ r-2 & =0 \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/id #uuid "5ee14499-89b0-59a3-9f13-b0a00c0e518d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18853" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ {{(r-9)}_{⏟}}_{a}z & ={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Constants on Both Sides"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203854 :question/id #uuid "36eadc0b-c2b0-5f84-8f39-2b8399932edb" :question/math-academy-id "e-17214" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2nx + 7  = -4x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204177}]}
